@@ -0,0 +1,40 @@
+# Check your repository's agent guidance
+
+`repo scan` reads the instruction files in a repository (`AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`,
+`.github/copilot-instructions.md`, ...) and checks every path, link, command and
+identifier they name against the tree. It makes no LLM call.
+
+## 1. Write a scan-only manifest
+
+Save this as `repo-eval.yaml` at the root of your repository:
+
+```yaml
+schema: repo_manifest.v1
+id: my-repo
+eval_dir: .agent-eval          # required by the schema; a scan does not read it
+guidance:
+  globs: ["**/AGENTS.md", "**/CLAUDE.md"]
+entry_points: {grade: "true"}
+tasks: .agent-eval/tasks
+build_systems: [pytest]
+```
+
+## 2. Run the scan
+
+From the `dokimi/` directory of a dokimi checkout:
+
+```bash
+python cli.py repo scan /path/to/my-repo --manifest repo-eval.yaml
+python cli.py repo scan /path/to/my-repo --manifest repo-eval.yaml --json
+```
+
+Without the file in the tree, `--manifest-file <local.yaml>` reads it from elsewhere.
+
+## 3. Read the result
+
+Each claim is `ok`, `unresolved` (the scan could not decide, for example a path
+a build creates) or `fail` (the file names something that does not exist). Fix
+every `fail`: an agent that follows that line goes wrong. `--fail-on-fail` makes
+the command exit 1 on any `fail`, for a CI gate.
+
+The full rules are in [repo-evaluation-contract.md](repo-evaluation-contract.md) §8.
